1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
ReadyRoute: A clear plan before. A clearer picture during.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,250
Emergencies create two problems at once. Households need a plan they can find quickly, and

3
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:16,750
responders need requests they can compare. People who face transport, language, or power

4
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:23,250
barriers may be especially hard to reach. ReadyRoute is our working, offline prototype for those gaps.

5
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:29,000
It has three views. First, a household saves an out-of-area contact, meeting place, backup

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,000
route, and access needs. Second, someone makes a short report with a location, need type,

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,500
urgency, and barriers. A compact relay code can carry that request to another device by hand

8
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:48,000
when devices cannot sync. Third, a coordinator sees an explained queue and can assign a resource.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Here is the household flow. In My plan, I enter sample details for the Rivera household: Elena

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,500
as a contact, North library as a meeting point, and an accessible van as a backup. I save it

11
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:07,750
locally and can print a copy. There is no account. The interface also has larger text and a partial Spanish option.

12
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:13,000
Now consider the response view. A fictional request for medical support at Pine Avenue

13
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,250
apartments says help is needed right now and power-dependent equipment is involved. The queue

14
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:24,250
shows thirty points for urgency, ten for an essential need, and six for an access barrier.

15
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:29,000
ReadyRoute reserves the medical backup-power team for that request. A separate general medical

16
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,500
team remains available for a request that does not need power support. The coordinator chooses

17
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:39,750
whether to assign, which reduces capacity. The app never dispatches help on its own.

18
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:45,250
The concept could make intake faster, explain prioritization more clearly, and preserve scarce

19
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:51,250
specialist support. The relay code omits free-text details but includes a location, so it

20
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:57,750
should only be shared with someone trusted. It is not encrypted. We have not measured

21
00:01:57,750 --> 00:02:03,000
real-world impact. A real pilot would need official data, secure synchronization, consent,

22
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
community testing, and emergency-management partners. This is not an official warning or dispatch system.
